  "prompt_text": "This figure is a conceptual cross-section showing how ecosystems and microbes change along a thaw gradient, from intact permafrost on the left to fully thawed ground on the right. In the frozen state, snow overlies a shallow active layer above ice-rich permafrost, and the subsurface contains dormant microbial hosts and viruses with low activity. As thaw progresses, the active layer thickens and the ground subsides where ground ice melts. Vegetation also becomes larger and denser, shifting from sparse low plants to shrubs and then wetland-type plants near standing water.\n\nThe biological message is that warming and thaw stimulate belowground life. In the partially thawed zone, microbes and viruses appear more abundant and diverse, with labels indicating increased host growth and activity. Farther into the thawed landscape, a visible water table develops and plant communities change again, accompanied by shifts in microbial hosts and viral populations. Overall, the image links physical thaw, hydrology, and plant succession with a transition from frozen, low-activity microbial systems to more active, restructured host–virus communities in wetter, thawed soils.",
